B|MCQ|Which statement best captures the purpose of cellular transcription?|Copy every chromosome before division|Produce RNA from selected DNA information|Convert DNA directly into protein|Replace both parental strands|B|Transcription produces RNA from a selected template region. Whole-chromosome duplication is replication; protein synthesis is translation, and parental DNA is retained.
B|MCQ|Which substrate set normally supports DNA-directed RNA synthesis?|dATP, dGTP, dCTP, dTTP|AMP, GMP, CMP, UMP|ATP, GTP, CTP, UTP|Adenine, guanine, cytosine, uracil alone|C|Ribonucleoside triphosphates supply the ribose and activated phosphate. Deoxynucleotides are DNA substrates; monophosphates and free bases lack the usual activated triphosphate.
B|MCQ|The nucleophile in a standard elongation reaction is associated with which group?|Growing RNA 3′ hydroxyl|Template DNA 5′ phosphate|Incoming nucleotide 2′ hydroxyl|Released pyrophosphate|A|The terminal RNA 3′ oxygen attacks the incoming alpha phosphorus. DNA supplies sequence information, while the 2′ hydroxyl and leaving PPi do not form the new bond.
B|MCQ|Which phosphate of an ordinary elongating NTP remains in the RNA backbone?|Gamma only|Beta only|All three|Alpha|D|Alpha is closest to the sugar and becomes the internucleotide phosphate; beta and gamma are released together as PPi.
B|MCQ|A template is written 3′-GATCC-5′. Which primary RNA is correct?|5′-GAUCC-3′|5′-CUAGG-3′|5′-GGAUC-3′|5′-GGATC-3′|B|Read the template left to right, selecting C, U, A, G, G. A resembles the template rather than its complement; C reverses the answer; D contains thymine.
B|MCQ|The template is 5′-TACCG-3′. Which RNA is written correctly?|5′-AUGGC-3′|5′-UACCG-3′|5′-CGGUA-3′|5′-CGGUA-3′ with thymine replacing uracil|C|Read from the rightmost template base G toward T and complement to C, G, G, U, A. A complements without reversing; B merely substitutes U for T.
B|MCQ|A non-template strand reads 3′-ATGCA-5′. What is the primary RNA?|5′-UACGU-3′|5′-AUGCA-3′|5′-UGCAU-3′|5′-ACGUA-3′|D|Reverse the coding-strand notation to 5′-ACGTA-3′ and replace T by U. Complementing the strand would select the wrong template relationship.
B|MCQ|RNA polymerase normally reads the template in which direction?|3′ to 5′|5′ to 3′|Alternating directions at every nucleotide|Direction depends on whether RNA encodes protein|A|Antiparallel pairing requires 3′ to 5′ template reading for 5′ to 3′ RNA synthesis. Coding potential is irrelevant.
B|MCQ|Which term denotes the first copied DNA position?|Translation start codon|Transcription start site|Terminator|Replication origin|B|The first incorporated nucleotide corresponds to +1. Translation start, termination and replication initiation are distinct biological events.
B|MCQ|Which is true of gene-relative transcription coordinates?|A coordinate zero separates −1 and +1|Every promoter begins at −35|The position immediately upstream of +1 is −1|Downstream always means larger chromosome coordinates|C|There is no zero in this convention; promoter structures vary and leftward genes move toward smaller genomic coordinates.
B|MCQ|A functional RNA need not be translated because which example acts directly as RNA?|A protein enzyme|A DNA polymerase|A histone|Transfer RNA|D|tRNA participates in translation as RNA. The other examples name proteins, not RNA products.
B|MCQ|What explains primer independence of standard transcription?|The first NTP supplies a 3′ hydroxyl to form the first linkage|RNA lacks phosphodiester bonds|RNA polymerase works without a DNA template|The first nucleotide is always a DNA primer|A|De novo initiation aligns the first two NTPs. The first nucleotide supplies the hydroxyl, while DNA still provides the template for ordinary cellular transcription.
B|MCQ|Which feature is characteristic of a productive transcription bubble?|Both DNA strands copied into separate RNAs by one polymerase|The entire RNA remains paired to DNA|A locally opened DNA region with a short RNA-DNA hybrid|Permanent conversion of DNA into RNA|C|The bubble moves with the enzyme; older RNA emerges and DNA reanneals behind it. This differs from replication or permanent strand separation.
B|MCQ|Which observation best distinguishes a high elongation rate from high processivity?|High number of promoter copies|Long uninterrupted product formation before dissociation|Large nucleotide concentration alone|High protein abundance alone|B|Processivity is persistence through successive additions. Speed is measured as additions per unit time, not simply by the presence of long products.
B|MCQ|Which role do Mg²⁺ ions normally play in RNA polymerization?|They replace ribose in the chain|They determine the reading frame|They are incorporated once per residue|They help organize catalysis and stabilize charged groups|D|Catalytic metal ions facilitate the chemistry; they are not stoichiometric backbone constituents or translational signals.
B|MCQ|A typical primary transcript initiated with ATP retains which ATP-derived moiety at the unmodified 5′ end?|5′ triphosphate|Only gamma phosphate without ATP residue|A DNA primer|A peptide|A|The initiating ATP retains its triphosphate in the usual de novo product. Later NTPs lose beta and gamma during incorporation; processing may change the end.
B|MCQ|Which is a direct effect expected from weakening a functional promoter without changing the copied interval?|Every RNA must contain a new codon|Reduced productive initiation under matched conditions|Both strands are destroyed|The RNA is synthesized 3′ to 5′|B|A promoter can alter initiation probability without changing the primary sequence of RNAs that still start at the same site. None of the other outcomes follows.
B|MCQ|Which observation most directly supports readthrough?|A smaller protein|An intact upstream promoter motif|RNA continuous across the usual termination region into downstream DNA|An increase in total DNA|C|Readthrough requires transcription beyond an expected endpoint. Protein size and DNA amount are indirect, and a motif alone does not demonstrate transcription.
B|MCQ|A transcription error differs from a DNA mutation principally because it normally affects what?|Every chromosome permanently|Both strands of the genome|All future daughter cells|The particular RNA product and its derivatives|D|An error in RNA need not alter DNA. It can affect multiple proteins made from that RNA, but ordinary genome inheritance is not directly changed.
B|MCQ|Which feature distinguishes usual replication from transcription?|Both parental DNA strands are copied to DNA during replication|RNA polymerase synthesizes toward its 5′ end|Replication requires uracil in every product|Transcription requires an obligatory lagging RNA strand|A|Replication copies both strands to DNA; the other options misstate RNA direction, substrates or replication-fork terminology.
B|MCQ|What did the 1961 Brenner–Jacob–Meselson study support?|Every newly expressed protein requires newly synthesized ribosomes|RNA cannot associate with ribosomes|New RNA can associate with pre-existing ribosomes|DNA itself is the ribosomal product|C|The isotope/density distinction supported an RNA intermediate used by existing ribosomes. It did not imply that all RNAs are unstable or establish every step of transcription chemistry.
B|MCQ|A single steady-state RNA abundance measurement is insufficient to determine what?|Whether an RNA is a nucleic acid|Whether synthesis or removal changed|Whether DNA has a sugar|Whether RNA possesses a 3′ end|B|Abundance reflects the balance of production and removal. Either mechanism can increase the amount; direct synthesis and decay measurements are needed to separate them.
B|AR|Assertion: RNA is extended at its 3′ end. Reason: The growing chain's terminal 3′ hydroxyl participates in addition of the incoming nucleotide.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|A|The chemical group directly explains extension at the 3′ terminus. The other interpretations contradict the normal polymerization mechanism.
B|AR|Assertion: A promoter can affect RNA yield without being copied. Reason: RNA polymerase selects ribonucleotides by complementarity to template bases.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|B|Both statements are true, but the promoter affects recruitment or initiation; complementarity explains sequence copying, not the regulatory effect of an untranscribed promoter.
B|AR|Assertion: Every AUG in an RNA is its transcription start site. Reason: Transcription and translation recognize different molecular signals.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|D|AUG may serve translation initiation in the right context, while +1 is defined by RNA synthesis. The assertion is false.
B|AR|Assertion: A standard de novo primary RNA may have a 5′ triphosphate. Reason: The initiating NTP loses beta and gamma phosphates during its initial placement.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|C|The initial NTP retains its 5′ triphosphate in the standard unprocessed product. PPi release concerns the incoming second and later nucleotides.
B|AR|Assertion: Two genes on one DNA duplex may use opposite template strands. Reason: Opposite promoter orientations can direct transcription along opposite strands.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|A|Promoter direction determines which DNA strand is read 3′ to 5′ along the path. The reason provides the mechanism.
B|Multiple statement|Choose all correct statements: I. +1 is the transcription start. II. A promoter is always entirely upstream. III. The primary transcript may include untranslated sequences. IV. Every stop codon is a terminator.|I and II|II and IV|I and III|III and IV|C|I and III are correct. Some promoter elements overlap downstream sequence, and a translation stop codon is not a general transcription termination signal.
B|Multiple statement|Which statements hold for standard elongation? I. RNA grows at its 3′ end. II. PPi is released. III. The incoming NTP retains all three phosphates in the backbone. IV. Template RNA is required in normal DNA-directed synthesis.|I and II|I and III|II and IV|III and IV|A|The terminal 3′ end attacks alpha phosphate, releasing PPi. III misstates phosphate retention and IV confuses DNA-directed transcription with RNA-dependent RNA synthesis.
B|Multiple statement|Which statements are correct? I. A pause can be reversible. II. Every short RNA demonstrates termination. III. DNA may reanneal behind polymerase. IV. Protein output uniquely measures initiation.|I and IV|II and III|II and IV|I and III|D|Pauses may resume and local opening is reversed behind the complex. Short RNA can also result from stalling or cleavage; protein is a downstream measure.
B|Matching|Match 1. Promoter, 2. +1, 3. Stop codon, 4. Terminator with a. Translation termination, b. Initiation control region, c. First transcribed base, d. Transcription endpoint signal.|1-c, 2-b, 3-d, 4-a|1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d|1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c|1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b|B|The promoter organizes initiation, +1 is the first copied position, stop codon is a translation signal, and terminator is associated with ending transcription.
B|Matching|Match 1. Replication, 2. Transcription, 3. Reverse transcription, 4. Translation with a. RNA to protein, b. DNA to RNA, c. RNA to DNA, d. DNA to DNA.|1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c|1-a, 2-c, 3-d, 4-b|1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a|1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d|C|The mappings identify template and product. They represent information transfer rather than direct material conversion.
C|Numerical|A standard unprocessed RNA contains 1200 residues. How many internucleotide bonds and PPi releases occur during synthesis from free NTPs?|1200 and 1200|1199 and 1199|1199 and 1200|1200 and 1199|B|The first NTP initiates and each of 1199 later NTPs creates one bond and releases one PPi. Counting the first nucleotide as an elongation event creates the off-by-one error.
C|Numerical|An 18-nt template region contains 5 A, 4 T, 6 G and 3 C. What is the RNA U:G ratio?|4:6|5:6|4:3|5:3|D|Template A directs U and template C directs G. Therefore 5 U and 3 G are incorporated. The other ratios incorrectly use template bases without complementing.
C|Numerical|A leftward gene starts at genomic coordinate 900. What are the genomic coordinates of its +41 and −12 positions?|860 and 912|940 and 888|859 and 911|941 and 912|A|Positive +41 lies 40 bases downstream, toward decreasing coordinates: 900−40=860. Negative −12 is 12 upstream, toward increasing coordinates: 900+12=912.
C|Numerical|A contiguous DNA interval is labeled −17 through +43. How many bases does it contain, and how many are in the RNA if copying begins at +1 and stops at +43?|61 and 43|60 and 44|60 and 43|59 and 42|C|There are 17 upstream positions and 43 positive positions, totaling 60 because zero is absent. Only the 43 positive positions are copied.
C|Numerical|A 200-nt nascent chain grows to 980 nt at 39 additions/s, without pauses. How much further time is required?|25.13 s|20 s|30 s|5.13 s|B|Remaining additions are 980−200=780; 780/39=20 s. Dividing total length by rate counts already synthesized RNA.
C|Numerical|An idealized 1500-nt transcript takes 25 s of active extension and 5 s of pausing. Approximate active and overall average rates?|50 and 60 nt/s|60 and 60 nt/s|50 and 50 nt/s|60 and 50 nt/s|D|Active rate≈1500/25=60. Total time=30 s, so effective rate≈50. Pauses affect the average but not the specified active rate.
C|Numerical|A gene initiates every 4 s and takes 40 s per completed transcript in a steady, uncrowded pipeline. What is the idealized mean number of active complexes and output rate?|10 complexes; 0.25 transcripts/s|4 complexes; 10 transcripts/s|40 complexes; 4 transcripts/s|0.1 complexes; 40 transcripts/s|A|Residence time multiplied by initiation flux gives 40×(1/4)=10 complexes. With no losses, steady completion flux equals initiation flux, 0.25/s.
C|Numerical|A defined termination assay observes 90 ended RNAs and 30 readthrough products, all equally detectable and derived from one initiation site. What is the termination efficiency?|25%|30%|75%|90%|C|90/(90+30)=0.75. The readthrough fraction is 25%; 90 is the count, not the percentage.
C|Numerical|A species is produced at 12 molecules/cell/min and removed with k=0.3 min⁻¹. Under the first-order steady-state model, what is its abundance?|3.6 molecules/cell|40 molecules/cell|12.3 molecules/cell|0.025 molecules/cell|B|At steady state, R=s/k=12/0.3=40. Multiplication yields incompatible units; the reciprocal gives the wrong quantity.
C|Numerical|If the hypothetical independent error probability is 2×10⁻⁵ per residue for 500 residues, what is the approximate probability of at least one error?|0.002%|50%|10%|0.995%|D|The exact probability is 1−(1−0.00002)^500≈0.0099503, or 0.995%. The small-Lp approximation is 1%; per-residue probability alone is insufficient.
C|Numerical|A primer spans RNA positions +61 to +80, with its 3′ end paired to +61. Complete extension reaches +6. What is the DNA product length?|75 nt|80 nt|55 nt|74 nt|A|Extension copies +60 down through +6 inclusive, 55 bases. Add the 20-residue primer to obtain 75; 55 excludes the primer.
C|Analytical|Two genes cover the same DNA interval 5′-AGTCGA-3′ on the plus-reference strand, but transcribe rightward and leftward. What are their respective primary RNAs?|AGUCGA and AGUCGA|UCAGCU and AGUCGA|AGUCGA and UCGACU|UCAGCU and UCGACU|C|The rightward RNA matches the plus-reference sequence with U replacing T. The leftward RNA is its reverse complement, UCGACU. Same interval does not imply same strand.
C|Analytical|In a defined assay, the WT and mutant generate identical numbers of 8-nt initiated chains. WT makes 90 full-length RNAs and mutant makes 10. Which interpretation is best?|Mutant cannot recognize any promoter|A defect after the measured early initiation step is supported|All mutant DNA is absent|The full-length RNAs prove a translation defect|B|Early initiation remains detectable; divergence in full-length product points to a later transition, elongation, release or product stability. More measurements are needed to locate the precise defect.
C|Analytical|A reaction with polymerase, DNA and NTPs yields label in a high-molecular-weight fraction. Which control most directly tests whether that fraction contains RNA?|Adding excess unrelated protein|Counting promoter motifs|Repeating without ribosomes only|Testing susceptibility to an appropriate RNase against controls|D|RNase sensitivity supports product identity. Template-dependence controls are separately needed to demonstrate DNA-directed synthesis; ribosomes are not required for the polymerization reaction.
C|Analytical|Only gamma-labeled GTP is supplied as a tracer. A primary RNA starts with A and contains several internal G residues. What is predicted for an unprocessed standard product?|No gamma label retained through ordinary incorporation|Every G residue retains gamma label|Only the final G retains gamma label|All four bases become labeled|A|Internal GTP loses beta and gamma as PPi. A GTP start could retain gamma label, but the specified initiating base is A. This assumes no secondary exchange or modification.
C|Analytical|A −25 mutation changes RNA output from 100 to 20 molecules/min while the mapped +1 and product sequence remain unchanged. Which inference is most defensible?|The −25 base must appear in every RNA|The mutant now transcribes 3′ to 5′|A change in productive initiation is consistent with the data|Translation must begin at −25|C|The upstream mutation is not copied in the stated transcript. The unchanged start and sequence support a regulatory effect, although downstream stability controls are needed to prove altered initiation.
C|Analytical|A 200-nt RNA band accumulates when a required NTP is absent. Upon restoration, the labeled short chains become 600-nt products. Which model is favored?|Irreversible termination at 200|Retained complexes resumed extension|A DNA replication origin at 200|Translation of the missing NTP|B|Conversion of the previously labeled short RNA to longer product supports reversible arrest, assuming the pulse/chase tracks the same chains. Released terminal products would not normally extend in the same complex.
C|Analytical|A mutation increases polymerase occupancy along a gene but decreases completed RNA output. Which explanation is compatible with both observations?|Occupancy proves increased completion rate|All polymerases move faster|DNA is converted into protein|Longer residence or stalling with fewer productive completions|D|Occupancy reflects residence and entry. Accumulated stalled complexes can raise occupancy while lowering completion. Neither occupancy alone nor protein measures reveal the exact flux.
C|Analytical|A downstream probe detects a 900-nt species after a terminator mutation. An upstream probe detects a 500-nt species. What additional evidence is most useful for demonstrating readthrough?|Evidence that the 900-nt RNA also contains upstream sequence in the same molecule|A larger cell size|The presence of an AUG|An RNase-free buffer alone|A|Readthrough requires continuity from upstream initiation through the normal endpoint. The downstream RNA might instead originate at an independent promoter, so size and strand-specific sequence continuity matter.
C|Analytical|A promoter and its entire transcription unit are inverted together in a simplified context. What is the expected change?|RNA must be synthesized 3′ to 5′|All coding bases are replaced with complementary RNA bases relative to their own gene|Chromosomal direction reverses, while intrinsic primary RNA sequence can remain unchanged|Transcription and replication become identical|C|An intact inverted block preserves internal promoter–gene orientation while changing direction relative to the reference chromosome. The prediction assumes no contextual disruption.
C|Analytical|A protein becomes much less abundant after a start codon mutation, but primary RNA production and length are unchanged. What conclusion is best supported?|The DNA template cannot be recognized|A downstream defect in translation is plausible|The transcription start is always AUG|RNA polymerase has lost Mg²⁺ binding|B|Unchanged primary transcription argues against a major initiation defect in the measured conditions. A start codon mutation can affect translation; protein stability could also contribute.
C|Analytical|Two RNA species have the same steady-state abundance. Species X has half the removal constant of Y. In the simple model, what follows?|X has twice the synthesis rate of Y|Their synthesis rates must be equal|X cannot be transcribed|X has half the synthesis rate of Y|D|At steady state s=kR. With equal R and kX=kY/2, sX=sY/2. Equal abundance does not imply equal production.
C|Analytical|A reproducible RNA sequence difference is absent from the corresponding genomic DNA. Which conclusion is justified first?|Further controls are needed to distinguish RNA editing, copying error and analytical artifact|A heritable DNA mutation has been demonstrated|Every transcript must contain the difference|The promoter is necessarily inverted|A|An RNA–DNA difference warrants investigation but is not by itself diagnostic of editing or transcription error. Independent sample and method controls are required.